      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i checked the soil and it actually feels pretty wet right now, even though i only watered it a few days ago. could it be a drainage issue then? also is there a specific type of fertilizer i should look for if it's a nutrient thing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Excess moisture can lead to root rot and yellowing leaves. If the soil feels wet, it's possible that your pot has poor drainage, causing water to accumulate and Leading to root issues. Consider repotting your Monstera in a pot with good drainage holes, and use a potting mix that's specifically designed to prevent waterlogging.\n\nFor fertilization, a balanced, water-soluble fertilizer (20-20-20) is a good starting point. You can also consider a fertilizer with micronutrients like iron, which is often lacking in houseplants. Avoid over-fertilizing, and start with a weaker solution (half the recommended strength) to prevent burning your plant's roots.",
